}

{
  "title": "Why the sky is blue",
  "screens": [
    {
      "title": "A familiar color",
      "text": "On a clear day, the sky looks blue—even though sunlight looks nearly white. The change happens as sunlight passes through the air around Earth.",
      "imagePrompt": "White sunlight entering Earth's atmosphere above a person looking at a blue sky"
    },
    {
      "title": "Light has many colors",
      "text": "Sunlight is a mix of colors. Each color travels in waves of a different length: blue waves are shorter than red ones, like tighter ripples in water.",
      "imagePrompt": "Sunlight shown as a band of colors with short blue waves and longer red waves"
    },
    {
      "title": "Air spreads blue light",
      "text": "Tiny molecules in the air bump sunlight in different directions. They spread the shorter blue waves around more than the longer red waves. This is called scattering.",
      "imagePrompt": "Sunlight rays passing air molecules, with blue rays spreading in many directions and red rays staying straighter"
    },
    {
      "title": "Blue comes from everywhere",
      "text": "Some scattered blue light reaches your eyes from all across the sky, so the whole sky seems blue. Violet scatters even more, but our eyes notice blue better and sunlight has less violet.",
      "imagePrompt": "A person beneath a blue sky, with scattered blue rays reaching their eyes from many directions"
    },
    {
      "title": "Sunsets look different",
      "text": "At sunset, sunlight travels through more air to reach you. Much of its blue light gets scattered away, leaving more red and orange light to reach your eyes.",
      "imagePrompt": "Sunset near the horizon with a long path through the atmosphere and red-orange light reaching a viewer"
    }
  ],
  "check": {
    "question": "At sunset, the Sun looks orange. Based on how air affects sunlight, why?",
    "options": [
      {
        "text": "More blue light is scattered away before sunlight reaches you.",
        "isCorrect": true,
        "feedback": "Right. Near sunset, sunlight travels through more air, which scatters much of its blue light away."
      },
      {
        "text": "The air adds orange light to sunlight as the Sun sets.",
        "isCorrect": false,
        "feedback": "Not quite. The air does not add orange light; it scatters blue light away, leaving more red and orange."
      },
      {
        "text": "Red light is scattered more strongly than blue light.",
        "isCorrect": false,
        "feedback": "Not quite. Air scatters blue light more strongly, while more red and orange light continues toward you."
      }
    ]
  },
  "recap": [
    "Sunlight is a mix of colors with different wave lengths.",
    "Air scatters shorter blue waves more than longer red ones.",
    "Scattered blue light makes the sky blue; sunsets leave more red and orange."
  ],
  "goFurther": {
    "overviewCourse": "Earth Science",
    "relatedQuestions": [
      "Why do clouds look white?",
      "Why does the Moon sometimes look orange?",
      "How does a rainbow form?"
    ]
  }
}

{
  "title": "Como funciona uma vacina",
  "screens": [
    {
      "title": "Um treino antes do encontro",
